<FieldGuideLayout
  title="Overlay knowledgebase"
  description="The high-level shape of the ~170 ARM9 overlays — what they are, how they load, and why patching them is delicate. The full per-overlay catalog is being assembled separately."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'How the Game Works', href: `${base}/field-guide/how-the-game-works/` },
    { label: 'Overlay knowledgebase' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    This page is the "what's an overlay" explainer. The
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/how-the-game-works/overlays/`}>overlay system</a>
    page already covers the technical specifics (RAM slots, controller/body
    pattern, persistent engines). What's missing — and being built now — is a
    per-overlay catalog: one row per overlay, with a confirmed role, a list of
    notable functions, and a note on whether patching it is known to be safe.
    That catalog will be linked from this page when it's ready.
  </p>

  <h2>What is an overlay?</h2>
  <p>
    The DS has very little RAM (4 MB total) and Tongari Boushi is a fairly
    large game. To fit, the engine breaks its code into <strong>overlays</strong>:
    chunks of compiled ARM9 code that are loaded from the cartridge into a
    shared RAM region only when they're needed. When the player moves between
    game modes — exiting a shop, opening the mail box, entering the cooking
    minigame — the engine evicts the current overlay and loads the next one
    into the same address.
  </p>
  <p>
    There are around <strong>170 overlays</strong> in this game (180 entries
    in the table, with a small number unused). A handful are resident the
    whole time the game is running (the 2D rendering engine
    <code>ov137</code> is the big one); the rest swap in and out.
  </p>

  <h2>How an overlay loads</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>The state machine in the main ARM9 binary decides "we're now in mode X."</li>
    <li>It looks up overlay X in the overlay table at ROM offset <code>0x8DE00</code>.</li>
    <li>That table row says: load file <code>overlay/overlay_NNNN.bin</code>, decompress if needed, and copy it to address <code>ram_addr</code>.</li>
    <li>If the destination RAM slot already holds a different overlay, that overlay is overwritten.</li>
    <li>The new overlay's entrypoint runs; it sets up state, registers event handlers, and yields back to the main loop.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Why patching overlays is delicate</h2>
  <p>
    Most of our v2.x release work happens inside container files
    (<code>data/*</code>) — the dialog strings, item tables, magic glyphs.
    Those are safe to patch because the engine reads them at runtime and
    doesn't care about their internal layout as long as the file format is
    valid.
  </p>
  <p>
    Overlays are different. An overlay is <em>compiled code</em>. It has
    hard-coded addresses, fixed-stride data tables, and pointers into
    <code>.rodata</code> that were resolved at link time. If you change a
    string or a constant inside an overlay you have to either:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Keep the patch byte-exact in length</strong> — replace 4 bytes
      with exactly 4 bytes, never more — so nothing else in the overlay shifts; or</li>
    <li><strong>Recompute every pointer in the overlay</strong> that pointed
      past your edit, which means re-resolving the relocation table by hand.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Get either of those wrong and the game crashes, usually as soon as the
    overlay is loaded. So overlay edits are surgical: the patch tools track
    each overlay edit as a specific (file, offset, before-bytes, after-bytes)
    tuple and refuse to apply if the original bytes don't match.
  </p>

  <h2>What v2.31 changed</h2>
  <p>
    Version <strong>2.31</strong> of the patch touches six overlays:
    <code>ov047</code>, <code>ov059</code>, <code>ov065</code>,
    <code>ov096</code>, <code>ov170</code>, <code>ov171</code>. Each edit is
    a small, length-preserving string or constant change. The full list of
    bytes changed lives in the patch metadata; see
    <a href={`${base}/patches/`}>the patches page</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Of the other ~164 overlays, none have been edited yet. Most are read-only
    in the patch sense — we know what they do but we don't need to change them.
    A handful are still labeled "TBD" in our internal notes; those are the
    targets the per-overlay catalog work is filling in next.
  </p>

